---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE_NAME, SITE_TITLE, RELATIONS, pageHref } from '../lib/site';
---
<Base title={`Method · ${SITE_NAME}`}>
  <header class="wrap site-head">
    <a class="site-name" href={pageHref()}>{SITE_NAME}</a>
    <nav class="site-nav" aria-label="Site">
      <a href={pageHref()}>All isms and themes</a>
      <a href={pageHref('about')}>About</a>
    </nav>
  </header>
  <main id="main" class="wrap">
    <article class="prose">
      <h1>Method</h1>
      <h2>What counts as an ism</h2>
      <p>An ism is mapped here when it passes three tests. It is a whole view of AI: what AI is, where it is heading and what we should do about it. It was born in the tech world, among people who build, fund or think inside the AI industry. And its own people use its name, with founding texts, a vocabulary and institutions that can be traced.</p>
      <h2>Families and themes</h2>
      <p>The isms are grouped in three families, as the debate usually groups them: transcendence, risk and acceleration. Themes are the subjects every ism has a view on, such as abundance or power. Themes have no timeline of their own; they compare how the isms answer them.</p>
      <h2>How roots are marked</h2>
      <p>Every root in a timeline says how it is connected to the ism today. The most important difference is between ancestors the movement claims for itself and ancestors that historians attribute to it.</p>
      <ul>
        {Object.values(RELATIONS).map((r) => <li>{r}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>Every entry names its source. Texts are first drafted, then checked claim by claim against those sources by a second reader. Entries not yet checked are marked as drafts.</p>
      <h2>Images</h2>
      <p>All images are made by people, never generated by AI. Each names its source and its rights. Images whose rights are not yet cleared are shown as labelled placeholders.</p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
